  <p>So, you have found out the wonderful news that you are carrying a little human. Congratulations! What an exciting time for you and your loved ones.</p>
  <p>While you are getting your head around it, pregnancy can also feel daunting, as if things are suddenly moving at a thousand miles an hour. You may have been preparing for this for a long time, or only since last week, or it may be a complete surprise. Whatever your circumstance, it is normal to feel responsible for the new life inside you, to not want to get anything wrong, and to want to do all you can so your baby grows and develops as they should.</p>
  <p>That is why Kat has put together this summary of the key things to know about nourishing your body well through pregnancy. Grab a drink and a comfy, quiet spot, and read on for the role good nutrition plays in supporting you and your baby.</p>

  <h2>Gestational diabetes support during pregnancy</h2>
  <p>Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) affects around 1 in 8 Australian pregnancies and is one of the most common complications of pregnancy. Personalised nutrition is the first-line treatment, and that is exactly where Kat comes in. As an Accredited Practising Dietitian with a focus on pregnancy nutrition, Kat works alongside your obstetric team to help you manage blood glucose through food, understand your carbohydrate portions, and build a realistic meal plan that nourishes both you and your baby. Read more about <a href="/our-services/pregnancy-nutrition-and-dietetics/gestational-diabetes/">gestational diabetes nutrition</a>.</p>

  <h2>Medicare rebates for dietitian appointments</h2>
  <p>You may be eligible for Medicare-rebated dietitian sessions. As an Accredited Practising Dietitian (APD), Kat's consultations can be claimed under a GP Chronic Condition Management Plan (GPCCMP). Eligible clients can access up to five rebated allied health sessions per calendar year, which reduces your out-of-pocket cost. Private health insurance extras may also cover dietitian appointments. Ask your GP whether you qualify.</p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <h3>Why see a pregnancy dietitian?</h3>
  <p>A pregnancy dietitian gives you personalised guidance so that both you and your baby receive the nutrition you need throughout pregnancy. Kat helps you build a tailored eating plan for your specific needs, from managing morning sickness to getting enough key nutrients such as folate, iron and calcium, so you can make informed food choices with confidence.</p>

  <h3>How does a baby receive nutrition in the womb?</h3>
  <p>Through the placenta. The placenta connects you and your baby: your bloodstream delivers oxygen and nutrients to the placenta, which passes them across to your baby's bloodstream, and carries waste products back to be cleared by your body. The placenta plays a vital role in providing your baby with what they need to grow and develop.</p>

  <h3>Are there foods that support blood flow to the placenta?</h3>
  <p>There is no specific list of foods that directly increase blood flow to the placenta, but a balanced, nutritious diet supports overall cardiovascular health, which in turn supports blood flow. Some things to consider:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A balanced diet.</strong> A variety of nutrient-dense foods: whole grains, lean proteins, healthy fats, fruit and vegetables, which provide the vitamins, minerals and antioxidants that support heart and blood vessel health.</li>
    <li><strong>Omega-3 fats.</strong> Oily fish such as salmon and sardines, walnuts, chia seeds and flaxseeds.</li>
    <li><strong>Citrus fruit.</strong> Oranges, grapefruit and lemons are rich in vitamin C, which supports blood vessel health.</li>
    <li><strong>Leafy greens.</strong> Spinach, kale and silverbeet are rich in nitrates, which help blood vessels widen.</li>
    <li><strong>Garlic.</strong> Associated with healthy circulation. Add fresh garlic to meals or use it as a seasoning.</li>
    <li><strong>Beetroot.</strong> Beetroot and beetroot juice also contain nitrates that support healthy blood vessels.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Talk to your healthcare provider or an Accredited Practising Dietitian for personalised advice, so your food choices fit your needs and any underlying health conditions.</p>

  <h3>How do I get enough iron during pregnancy?</h3>
  <p>Include iron-rich foods such as lean red meat, spinach, legumes and fortified cereals, and pair them with vitamin C-rich foods such as oranges or capsicum to improve absorption. Your body's demand for iron rises during pregnancy, especially in the second and third trimesters, so Kat recommends having your iron levels checked regularly at your antenatal appointments. If your levels are low, your GP or obstetrician may recommend a supplement.</p>

  <h3>How do I manage morning sickness and food aversions?</h3>
  <p>Nausea and food aversions are common in the first trimester and can make meeting your nutritional needs feel impossible. Practical, evidence-based strategies Kat recommends:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eat small, frequent meals.</strong> An empty stomach can worsen nausea. Aim for something every two to three hours, even a few crackers.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep dry crackers or plain toast by the bed.</strong> A small snack before getting up can take the edge off morning nausea.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose cold or room-temperature foods.</strong> Hot food carries stronger smells. Try cold sandwiches, yoghurt or fruit.</li>
    <li><strong>Ginger and vitamin B6.</strong> Both have good evidence for easing nausea. Talk to Kat or your GP about what is appropriate for you.</li>
    <li><strong>Stay hydrated.</strong> Sip water, herbal tea or diluted juice through the day. Dehydration makes nausea worse.</li>
    <li><strong>Avoid strong smells.</strong> Cold, bland foods are your friend in this phase. Do not pressure yourself to eat your usual diet if it is triggering aversions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If nausea is severe, causing weight loss, or you cannot keep fluids down (hyperemesis gravidarum), speak with your healthcare provider promptly. Kat can work alongside your medical team to support your nutrition through this.</p>

  <h3>What are the common nutrition concerns in pregnancy?</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Getting enough of the key nutrients.</strong> Folate, iron, calcium and omega-3 fats are particularly important. Folate helps prevent neural tube defects, iron supports red blood cell production and helps prevent anaemia, calcium is vital for your baby's bones, and omega-3 fats contribute to brain and eye development.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight gain.</strong> Appropriate weight gain supports a healthy pregnancy. The recommended range depends on your pre-pregnancy weight and varies from person to person, so work with your healthcare provider to set a goal and monitor it.</li>
    <li><strong>Food safety.</strong> Pregnancy increases the risk from foodborne illness. Higher-risk foods include raw or undercooked meat, fish high in mercury, unpasteurised dairy and some soft cheeses. Good storage, handling and cooking habits matter. See <a href="/blogs/foods-to-avoid-during-pregnancy-australia/">foods to avoid during pregnancy in Australia</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Talk to your healthcare provider or an Accredited Practising Dietitian for guidance based on your own nutritional needs.</p>

  <h3>What is gestational diabetes and how is it managed?</h3>
  <p>Gestational diabetes is a form of diabetes that develops during pregnancy when the body cannot produce enough insulin to meet increased demands. It affects around 1 in 8 Australian pregnancies and is usually diagnosed between 24 and 28 weeks with an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT).</p>
  <p>Nutrition is the cornerstone of GDM management. A personalised eating plan from an Accredited Practising Dietitian can help you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>spread carbohydrate across the day to avoid blood glucose spikes</li>
    <li>choose lower-GI carbohydrate sources that digest more slowly</li>
    <li>pair carbohydrate with protein and healthy fats to soften the glucose response</li>
    <li>meet your pregnancy nutrient needs while keeping blood glucose in range</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Many women with GDM manage their blood glucose through diet, with insulin added by their medical team if it is needed. If you have been diagnosed with GDM, book in with Kat as soon as you can. See <a href="/our-services/pregnancy-nutrition-and-dietetics/gestational-diabetes/">gestational diabetes nutrition</a>.</p>

  <h3>Can I claim Medicare for dietitian appointments?</h3>
  <p>In many cases, yes. As an APD, Kat's services may be eligible for a Medicare rebate under a GP Chronic Condition Management Plan (GPCCMP). Under this plan, eligible clients can access up to five Medicare-rebated allied health sessions per calendar year. Conditions that may qualify include gestational diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) and endometriosis, among other chronic conditions.</p>
  <p>To access the rebate:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>see your GP and ask whether you are eligible for a GP Chronic Condition Management Plan</li>
    <li>if you are, your GP refers you to Kat as an allied health provider</li>
    <li>after each consultation you receive a receipt to claim your rebate</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Private health extras may also cover dietitian sessions depending on your policy. It is worth checking both.</p>
